                    coach_default:          `Plan logged.`,
                    coach_r1_wastage:       `<strong>That is the real leak.</strong> Open-vial wastage in thin hill sessions makes effective supply far smaller than the 172,000 nominal doses — the counter-intuitive driver of the coverage gap. Now size your plan against effective, not nominal, supply.`,
                    coach_r1_pullers:       `<strong>Half right — the urban pull is real,</strong> but it is a symptom. The deeper leak is open-vial wastage shrinking effective supply before any routing decision is made. Find the supply you are losing, not just where it flows.`,
                    coach_r1_capacity:      `<strong>It is not raw vehicles.</strong> Capacity is 16% short, yes, but the bigger loss is wastage netting your effective supply down below 158,000. Solve the leak first; then the capacity gap is smaller than it looks.`,
                    coach_r1_staffing:      `<strong>This is very much a supply-chain problem.</strong> Staffing matters, but your barcode data points straight at open-vial wastage and the hill pull. Diagnose the network, or you will plan against a supply you do not actually have.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named wastage as the structural failure AND netted it out of nominal supply — so you are planning against ~158,000 children, the number that is actually true. That is the analytics edge SuryaHealth's data was built for.`,
                    coach_r1_nominal:       `<strong>You are planning on paper doses.</strong> Sizing against 172,000 nominal ignores the 8% (rising to 22%) wastage clock — effective supply is nearer 158,000 and falling. Net it out now or be blindsided when three more sites stock out.`,
                    coach_r1_optimistic:    `<strong>Hope is not a plan.</strong> Banking on the manufacturer making good the lot slip is how a short campaign becomes a failed one. Plan against effective supply you can actually count on, not 180,000 you might get.`,
                    coach_r2_equity:        `<strong>Equity-weighted service is the defensible choice.</strong> Protecting the hill blocks at 95% directly closes the 19-point gap the campaign exists to close — and you can say out loud what service level each child is getting.`,
                    coach_r2_uniform:       `<strong>Uniform 93% is even-handed but not equitable.</strong> Treating an already-behind hill block the same as an ahead urban ward leaves the structural gap roughly where it was. Even-handed input is not equitable outcome.`,
                    coach_r2_urban:         `<strong>This is throughput tunnel vision.</strong> 97% urban and 85% hill maximises doses shipped while widening the exact gap the funder is paying you to close. What service level are you implicitly assigning a hill-block child — and can you defend it out loud?`,
                    coach_r2_edgeright:     `<strong>Edge-weighted buffer is right here.</strong> The hill blocks are an 11-hour round trip and cannot be resupplied mid-week, so the protection has to sit with them — not in a central reserve they will never reach in time.`,
                    coach_r2_alledge:       `<strong>Almost all to the edge is a touch far.</strong> Edge stock protects the un-resuppliable hills, but with nothing central you cannot absorb a forecast error — and thin-session wastage rises. Keep a small flexible reserve.`,
                    coach_r2_central:       `<strong>Central buffer is elegant on paper.</strong> But it is the buffer your hill sites will never see in time — they are an 11-hour round trip with no mid-week resupply. Push more to the edge, knowing edge stock must be wastage-managed.`,
                    coach_r2_keep:          `<strong>10-dose vials in 4-child sessions is the wastage trap.</strong> You planned 10-dose vials for sessions averaging 4 children — wastage jumps toward 22% and your effective supply falls again. Right-size the vial or batch the session.`,
                    coach_r2_overship:      `<strong>Over-shipping does not fix wastage — it funds it.</strong> Pushing more 10-dose vials into thin hill sessions raises both wastage and cost per dose delivered. The lever is vial size and session batching, not volume.`,
                    coach_r2_lever:         `<strong>That is the wastage lever.</strong> Five-dose vials and batched caregivers in thin sessions lift effective supply and drop cost per dose — exactly where nominal and effective supply had diverged most. Paired with edge buffer, the hills are now genuinely protected.`,
                    coach_r3_mixed:         `<strong>Mixed routing respects the constraint.</strong> Hub-and-spoke for the plains keeps cost down, while the motorcycle cold-box leg gets the last hill kilometres inside the 4-hour window. You buy hill coverage without breaching the cold chain.`,
                    coach_r3_dedicated:     `<strong>Dedicated remote runs protect equity and the cold chain.</strong> They cost ₹0.62 lakh a cycle, but the doses arrive viable and the hill gap closes — and cost per dose delivered beats a cheap route that strands product.`,
                    coach_r3_hub:           `<strong>Hub-and-spoke on cost alone breaches the clock.</strong> Your milk-run reaches the last hill site at hour 5.5; the log shows a breach past hour 4, so those doses are non-viable on arrival. You saved ₹1.8 lakh in transport and destroyed ₹6 lakh of vaccine — what is your true cost per immunised child now?`,
                    coach_r3_toomany:       `<strong>You are over-buying cycles.</strong> Past a point, extra dedicated runs add cost faster than coverage and eat the ₹3.8 crore envelope. Four to ten cycles cover the hills; beyond that you are paying for diminishing returns.`,
                    coach_r3_nocycles:      `<strong>No dedicated cycles leaves the hills exposed.</strong> Without a remote run or a cold-box leg, the hill blocks are served last and at temperature risk — the equity gap will not close on the plains routing alone.`,
                    coach_r3_coldfail:      `<strong>You under-protected the cold chain.</strong> Skimp on refrigeration, dry-ice and route timing and doses breach the 4-hour window and arrive dead. Cold chain is a hard constraint, not a soft preference — and breaches show up as cost per dose delivered.`,
                    coach_r4_equity:        `<strong>An explicit equity rule is the right call.</strong> Prioritising the coverage-deficit hill child and outbreak risk for the last dose is a rule you can defend to the funder — and it sends the scarce dose to the child the campaign is behind on.`,
                    coach_r4_age:           `<strong>Age-based triage is clear but blind to equity.</strong> Youngest-first is defensible clinically, yet it ignores which children are behind on coverage and which blocks carry outbreak risk. It will not close the gap as fast as a deficit-plus-risk rule.`,
                    coach_r4_fcfs:          `<strong>First-come-first-served is a regressive rule.</strong> It systematically protects the mobile, urban, on-time families and fails exactly the hill children you are behind on. 'No rule' is the worst rule.`,
                    coach_r4_none:          `<strong>Refusing to write a rule IS a rule.</strong> It is day 24, three sites are down to 40 doses, 120 caregivers are queued and your staff are phoning you. 'Decide later' becomes first-come-first-served — regressive by default. Write the rule now: who gets the last dose, and why?`,
                    coach_r4_held:          `<strong>You held the rule the shock was built for.</strong> Reallocating toward the cluster under your equity rule is exactly the test — the rule, the inventory plan and the routing now hit the target together, not by luck.`,
                    coach_r4_freeze:        `<strong>Freezing and escalating loses precious days.</strong> Waiting on emergency doses you may not get stalls coverage while the cluster grows. A good triage rule lets you act inside your own supply rather than scramble for more.`,
                    coach_r4_abandon:       `<strong>Abandoning equity protects the headline, not the children.</strong> Dumping doses on the easy urban wards lifts the blended number while the hill blocks — your highest outbreak risk — fall further behind. That is the exact failure the campaign exists to prevent.`,
                    coach_r4_blow:          `<strong>You hit coverage at ₹4.4 crore.</strong> The overrun comes straight out of next quarter's routine immunisation runs — you borrowed coverage from next quarter's infants to pay for this campaign. Is this number now worth more than the routine coverage you just cut?`,

